            <tr><td>0.2</td><td>2/10</td><td>1/5</td><td>20%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.222...</td><td>≈ 222/1000</td><td><strong>2/9</strong></td><td>22.22%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.25</strong></td><td>25/100</td><td><strong>1/4</strong></td><td>25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.3</td><td>3/10</td><td>3/10</td><td>30%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.333...</td><td>≈ 333/1000</td><td><strong>≈ 1/3</strong></td><td>33.33%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.375</strong></td><td>375/1000</td><td><strong>3/8</strong></td><td>37.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.4</td><td>4/10</td><td>2/5</td><td>40%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.5</strong></td><td>5/10</td><td><strong>1/2</strong></td><td>50%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.6</td><td>6/10</td><td>3/5</td><td>60%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.625</strong></td><td>625/1000</td><td><strong>5/8</strong></td><td>62.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.666...</td><td>≈ 666/1000</td><td><strong>≈ 2/3</strong></td><td>66.67%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.6875</strong></td><td>6875/10000</td><td><strong>11/16</strong></td><td>68.75%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.75</strong></td><td>75/100</td><td><strong>3/4</strong></td><td>75%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.8</td><td>8/10</td><td>4/5</td><td>80%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.875</strong></td><td>875/1000</td><td><strong>7/8</strong></td><td>87.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.9</td><td>9/10</td><td>9/10</td><td>90%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sixteenths Table (popular searches) -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Fractions in Sixteenths — Popular in Engineering</h2>
      <p>The 16ths series is commonly searched for machining, woodworking, and measurement conversions.</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto">
        <table class="ref-tbl">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Decimal</th><th>Fraction</th><th>Percent</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0.0625</td><td>1/16</td><td>6.25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.125</td><td>2/16 = <strong>1/8</strong></td><td>12.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.1875</td><td>3/16</td><td>18.75%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.25</td><td>4/16 = <strong>1/4</strong></td><td>25%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.3125</strong></td><td><strong>5/16</strong></td><td>31.25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.375</td><td>6/16 = <strong>3/8</strong></td><td>37.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.4375</td><td>7/16</td><td>43.75%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.5</td><td>8/16 = <strong>1/2</strong></td><td>50%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.5625</td><td>9/16</td><td>56.25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.625</td><td>10/16 = <strong>5/8</strong></td><td>62.5%</td></tr>
            <tr class="hl"><td><strong>0.6875</strong></td><td><strong>11/16</strong></td><td>68.75%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.75</td><td>12/16 = <strong>3/4</strong></td><td>75%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.8125</td><td>13/16</td><td>81.25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.875</td><td>14/16 = <strong>7/8</strong></td><td>87.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>0.9375</td><td>15/16</td><td>93.75%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.5 as a fraction?</h3>
        <p>0.5 as a fraction is <strong>1/2</strong>. Write 0.5 as 5/10, then simplify: GCD(5,10)=5, so 5÷5 / 10÷5 = 1/2. In other words, 0.5 is exactly one half.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.75 as a fraction?</h3>
        <p>0.75 as a fraction is <strong>3/4</strong>. Write as 75/100, GCD(75,100)=25, so 75÷25 / 100÷25 = 3/4. That's three-quarters, or 75%.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.625 as a fraction?</h3>
        <p>0.625 as a fraction is <strong>5/8</strong>. Write as 625/1000, GCD(625,1000)=125, so 625÷125 / 1000÷125 = 5/8. This equals 62.5%.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.6875 as a fraction? (or "6875 as a fraction")</h3>
        <p>0.6875 as a fraction is <strong>11/16</strong>. Write as 6875/10000, GCD(6875,10000)=625, so 6875÷625 / 10000÷625 = 11/16. This is 68.75%. In engineering and measurement contexts, 11/16 is a standard fractional inch value.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.3125 as a fraction? (or "3125 as a fraction")</h3>
        <p>0.3125 as a fraction is <strong>5/16</strong>. Write as 3125/10000, GCD(3125,10000)=625, so 5/16. This equals 31.25%.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0.333 as a fraction?</h3>
        <p>0.333 (not repeating) as a fraction is <strong>333/1000</strong>. However, if you mean the repeating decimal 0.333… (1/3), that equals exactly 1/3. Since true 1/3 cannot be written as a finite decimal, entering 0.333 gives the closest finite approximation: 333/1000.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do you simplify a fraction using GCD?</h3>
        <p>GCD (Greatest Common Divisor) is the largest number that divides both numerator and denominator evenly. Example: GCD(625,1000). Since 625=5⁴ and 1000=2³×5³, GCD=5³=125. Divide both: 625÷125=5 and 1000÷125=8. Result: 5/8. Our calculator does this automatically using the Euclidean algorithm.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is a mixed number?</h3>
        <p>A mixed number combines a whole number with a proper fraction. For example, 1.75 = 7/4 as an improper fraction, but as a mixed number it's <strong>1 3/4</strong> (one and three-quarters). Mixed numbers are easier to read when the value is greater than 1. Our calculator shows the mixed number form in the stats panel.</p>
      </div>
